course: dictionary
lesson: 1706
title: A cigar in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cigar is a thick roll of dried tobacco leaves that people light and smoke. «The old man lit a cigar and puffed out a grey cloud of smoke.» The words lit, puffed and grey tell you that a cigar is something you set on fire and breathe smoke from.
explain: Some words are used in phrases, and the meaning of a phrase is not always the same as its single words. «Her answer was close but no cigar.» Nobody is talking about a real cigar here. The clue is close: the answer was nearly right, but not quite.
explain: Context also shows you what kind of people or places a word goes with. In stories and old films, a cigar often shows a rich businessman or a gangster. In health lessons, you learn that cigars contain tobacco and harm your health, so the context is different, but the meaning of the word stays the same.
fact: A cigar is a thick roll of dried tobacco leaves that people light and smoke.
fact: Cigars, like cigarettes, contain tobacco.
fact: Smoking cigars can cause cancers and heart disease.
fact: «Close but no cigar» means nearly right but not quite right.
fact: Phrases are groups of words with a special meaning, so check the clues around them.
exercise: «The old man lit a cigar and puffed out a grey cloud of smoke.» What is a cigar? | A small box for keeping pencils | *A thick roll of dried tobacco leaves that people light and smoke | A kind of grey cloud | A long, thin stick of sweets
exercise: «Her answer was close but no cigar.» What does this phrase mean? | Exactly right and perfect | A kind of cigar shop | Loud and cheerful | *Nearly right, but not quite
exercise: Health lessons teach that cigars are harmful. Why? | Because they are only for birthdays | Because they are made of chocolate | Because they make you grow taller | *Because they contain tobacco, which can cause cancers and heart disease
test: «The film gangster smoked a cigar in a dark room.» Why do old films often show a cigar with a rich businessman or a gangster? | Cigars are shown as a sign of a sunny holiday | *Films use cigars to suggest a rich or dangerous character | Films never show cigars at all | Gangsters only like sweets
test: «He said, close but no cigar, when we nearly won the match.» Swap in your own meaning for close but no cigar. Which one still makes sense? | We were the winners and the best team | We went to see a cigar shop | We ate all the cake | *We were nearly right, but we did not win
exam: Which is the best meaning of cigar? | A small bird that lives near the sea | A thick roll of dried tobacco leaves that people light and smoke | A kind of sweet | *A thick roll of dried tobacco leaves that people light and smoke, which can harm your health
